# Risk Policy

In this notebook you tune one typed risk policy that plain Elixir routing
code reads. `:anything` asks a proposal model for new policy values,
validates each one against the policy schema, and keeps the value that
routes validation cases correctly. You then save the winner as an artifact
and load it into a fresh program. The notebook runs offline with no
credentials.

## Setup

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:tune, "~> 0.5.0"},
  {:plug, "~> 1.20"}
])
```

## Script the models

`Scripted` serves OpenAI-compatible replies through an in-process Plug, so
ReqLLM runs its real request path without a network call. Each role gets a
reply function of the decoded request body. In real use you pass a real
model spec and the host's credentials instead.

```elixir
defmodule Scripted do
  @moduledoc "Serves scripted OpenAI-compatible replies to ReqLLM through an in-process Plug."

  def model, do: %{provider: :openai, id: "gpt-4-turbo", extra: %{wire: %{protocol: "openai_chat"}}}

  def options(reply) when is_function(reply, 1) do
    plug = fn conn ->
      {:ok, body, conn} = Plug.Conn.read_body(conn)
      respond(conn, reply.(JSON.decode!(body)))
    end

    [api_key: "scripted", req_http_options: [plug: plug], max_retries: 0]
  end

  def role(reply), do: [model: model(), adapter: Tune.Adapter.Chat, request_options: options(reply)]

  def chat(fields) do
    Enum.map_join(fields, "\n\n", fn {name, value} -> "[[ ## #{name} ## ]]\n#{value}" end) <>
      "\n\n[[ ## completed ## ]]"
  end

  def field(body, name) do
    text = body["messages"] |> Enum.filter(&(&1["role"] == "user")) |> List.last() |> Map.fetch!("content")

    case Regex.run(~r/\[\[ ## #{name} ## \]\]\n(.*?)(?=\n\n\[\[ ## |\n\nRespond with|\z)/s, text) do
      [_, value] -> value
      nil -> nil
    end
  end

  defp respond(conn, text) do
    send_json(conn, 200, %{
      id: "scripted",
      model: "gpt-4-turbo",
      choices: [%{message: %{role: "assistant", content: text}, finish_reason: "stop"}],
      usage: %{prompt_tokens: 10, completion_tokens: 5, total_tokens: 15}
    })
  end

  defp send_json(conn, status, body) do
    conn
    |> Plug.Conn.put_resp_content_type("application/json")
    |> Plug.Conn.send_resp(status, JSON.encode!(body))
  end
end
```

## Define the policy and the router

`RiskRouter` implements `Tune.Program` without any predictor. It declares
two `Tune.Parameter` values: `"policy"`, with a review threshold and a
queue, and `"standard_queue"`. `call/3` reads both with
`Tune.Execution.value/2` and routes in plain Elixir.

```elixir
defmodule RiskRouter do
  @moduledoc "Routes a risk score to a queue with a typed, tunable policy."
  @behaviour Tune.Program

  alias Tune.{Execution, Parameter, Signature}

  defstruct [:policy, :standard_queue]

  def new do
    schema = Zoi.object(%{"threshold" => Zoi.float(), "queue" => Zoi.string()})

    {:ok, policy} =
      Parameter.new(
        schema: schema,
        value: %{"threshold" => 0.9, "queue" => "risk-review"},
        description: "Risk routing policy"
      )

    {:ok, standard_queue} = Parameter.new(schema: Zoi.string(), value: "general")
    %__MODULE__{policy: policy, standard_queue: standard_queue}
  end

  @impl true
  def signature(_program) do
    {:ok, signature} = Signature.new(inputs: [risk: Zoi.float()], outputs: [queue: Zoi.string()])
    signature
  end

  @impl true
  def parameters(program), do: %{"policy" => program.policy, "standard_queue" => program.standard_queue}

  @impl true
  def put_parameters(program, parameters),
    do: %{program | policy: parameters["policy"], standard_queue: parameters["standard_queue"]}

  @impl true
  def call(_program, %{"risk" => risk}, execution) do
    {:ok, policy} = Execution.value(execution, "policy")
    {:ok, standard} = Execution.value(execution, "standard_queue")
    queue = if risk >= policy["threshold"], do: policy["queue"], else: standard
    {:ok, %{"queue" => queue}, execution}
  end
end
```

The schema guards every new value. A string threshold fails validation:

```elixir
{:error, error} =
  Tune.Parameter.put_value(RiskRouter.new().policy, %{"threshold" => "high", "queue" => "risk-review"})

Exception.message(error)
```

```text
"invalid parameter value: threshold: invalid type: expected float"
```

## Run one case with the original policy

The router makes no model calls, but `Tune.Runtime.run/3` still requires a
target model configuration. This one raises if it is ever called.

```elixir
never = Scripted.role(fn _body -> raise "the router makes no model calls" end)

{:ok, prediction} = Tune.Runtime.run(RiskRouter.new(), %{risk: 0.7}, model: never)
prediction.output
```

```text
%{"queue" => "general"}
```

A risk of 0.7 should go to review, but the original threshold of 0.9 sends
it to the general queue.

## Script the proposals

The proposal model returns two values in order: first a value with a string
threshold, then a valid value with a lower threshold. Each value is JSON
text in the `new_value` field.

```elixir
{:ok, queue} =
  Agent.start_link(fn ->
    [
      %{"threshold" => "high", "queue" => "risk-review"},
      %{"threshold" => 0.6, "queue" => "risk-review"}
    ]
  end)

{:ok, requests} = Agent.start_link(fn -> [] end)

proposal = fn body ->
  Agent.update(requests, &(&1 ++ [body]))
  value = Agent.get_and_update(queue, fn [next | rest] -> {next, rest} end)
  Scripted.chat(new_value: JSON.encode!(value))
end

:ok
```

## Tune the policy

The metric returns a score and feedback that names the expected queue.
`parameter: "policy"` limits the search to that one value, so
`"standard_queue"` stays fixed.

```elixir
row = fn id, risk, queue -> %{id: id, input: %{risk: risk}, expected: %{queue: queue}} end

study =
  Tune.new(RiskRouter.new(), model: never, proposal: Scripted.role(proposal))
  |> Tune.data(
    train: [row.("low", 0.2, "general"), row.("elevated", 0.7, "risk-review")],
    validation: [row.("moderate", 0.4, "general"), row.("high", 0.8, "risk-review")]
  )
  |> Tune.score(:routing, fn row, output ->
    if output == row.expected,
      do: %{score: 1, feedback: "correct queue"},
      else: %{score: 0, feedback: "expected #{row.expected["queue"]} at risk #{row.input["risk"]}"}
  end)
  |> Tune.search(:anything, parameter: "policy", max_iterations: 2, minibatch_size: 2)
  |> Tune.limit(seed: 2, model_calls: 6)

{:ok, winner} = Tune.tune(study)

IO.inspect(winner.value.policy.value, label: "policy")
IO.inspect(winner.best.evaluation.selected_score, label: "validation score")
IO.inspect(winner.calls, label: "calls")
```

```text
policy: %{"queue" => "risk-review", "threshold" => 0.6}
validation score: 1.0
calls: %{proposal: 2}
```

The proposal request includes the value's JSON Schema and the training
trajectories with their feedback:

```elixir
first = requests |> Agent.get(&hd/1)
schema = first |> Scripted.field("json_schema") |> JSON.decode!()
types = for {name, property} <- schema["properties"], do: {name, property["type"]}
IO.inspect({schema["type"], Enum.sort(schema["required"]), Enum.sort(types)})
IO.puts(Scripted.field(first, "examples_with_feedback"))
```

```text
{"object", ["queue", "threshold"], [{"queue", "string"}, {"threshold", "number"}]}
inputs: {"risk":0.2}

score: 1
feedback: correct queue

inputs: {"risk":0.7}

score: 0
feedback: expected risk-review at risk 0.7
```

The iteration log shows what happened to each proposal:

```elixir
Enum.map(
  winner.metadata.notes.iterations,
  &{&1.iteration, &1.accepted?, &1.parent_minibatch, &1.child_minibatch}
)
```

Each tuple is `{iteration, accepted?, parent_minibatch, child_minibatch}`:

```text
[{1, false, 1, nil}, {2, true, 1, 2}]
```

The first proposal failed schema validation, so it was discarded without a
minibatch run (`child_minibatch: nil`). The second routed both training
cases correctly, beat its parent, and then scored 1.0 on validation.

## Save and reload the winner

`Tune.dump/1` builds a versioned `Tune.Artifact`. `Tune.Artifact.encode/1`
turns it into canonical JSON that you can store anywhere.

```elixir
{:ok, artifact} = Tune.dump(winner)
{:ok, json} = Tune.Artifact.encode(artifact)

path = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "risk_policy.json")
File.write!(path, json)

{:ok, saved} = path |> File.read!() |> Tune.Artifact.decode()
{:ok, loaded} = Tune.load(saved, RiskRouter.new())

loaded.policy.value
```

```text
%{"queue" => "risk-review", "threshold" => 0.6}
```

`Tune.load/2` checks that the artifact was learned for the same program
contract as the fresh `RiskRouter`, validates each value against its schema,
and returns the updated program. The routing code stays in your module; the
artifact carries the parameter values, a target fingerprint, and
provenance such as the optimizer, seed, and call counts.

## Run the same case after loading

```elixir
{:ok, before} = Tune.Runtime.run(RiskRouter.new(), %{risk: 0.7}, model: never)
{:ok, after_load} = Tune.Runtime.run(loaded, %{risk: 0.7}, model: never)

IO.puts("before: #{before.output["queue"]}")
IO.puts("after:  #{after_load.output["queue"]}")
```

```text
before: general
after:  risk-review
```

